#
# `:existsNestedCorrelation` fixture: two `:p` subjects (`:s1`/`:s2`) plus one
# UNRELATED `:q` fact (`:n1 :q :m1`, on a third subject neither `:s1` nor `:s2`
# ever binds to). `FILTER EXISTS { FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?x :q ?y } }` nests a
# `NOT EXISTS` inside an `EXISTS`, both bodies being a group whose sole element is
# a `FILTER` (no BGP) — so each reduces to "does the unit row survive the inner
# filter". Since `?x`/`?y` are fresh (uncorrelated with `?s`/`?o`), the verdict is
# the SAME for every outer row: the one `:q` fact anywhere in the graph makes the
# inner `NOT EXISTS` false, so the unit row never survives, so `EXISTS` is false
# for both `:s1` and `:s2` — proving nested EXISTS/NOT EXISTS composition through
# filter-only groups, and that a fresh (non-outer) variable is never wrongly
# captured as if it were correlated.
@prefix : <https://example.org/exists#> .

:s1 :p :o1 .
:s2 :p :o2 .
:n1 :q :m1 .
